xpertise is in Java, J2EE, Spring, and front-end frameworks like Angular, React, and AngularJS. I have worked with a database such as Oracle, MySQL, and I have experience with both SQL and NoSQL databases like MongoDB and the NoSQL database. On the front-end side, I have used HTML, CSS, JavaScript, jQuery, Angular, and I have experience with cloud platforms like AWS and Azure, including using the services like Lambda, DynamoDB, and CloudFront.","Sure, so in my current project, I''m working with a developer. I''m working with a senior Java full-stack developer, where I manage and develop a solution for the complex home mortgage loan liquidation, ensuring the compliance with the bank''s servicing guidelines and federal regulation. So in this project, I''m working with Java with Angular, and on the backend, I use Java and Spring Boot to build and upgrade internal and external applications for the mortgage origination process. For the front-end, I develop user interface using Angular, focusing on creating efficient and component-based screens for both internal teams, as well as end users."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmox08kvr00acn20knggpkuwt', 'cmowy0o8g009tn20kyp1yqroq', 95, 100, 99, 82, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-08 14:22:50.161', '2026-05-08 14:22:50.161', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["I can''t hear anything.","OK.","No."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmox1z4cc00avn20kzds7jwzu', 'cmox1irdc00agn20kvi6wmgjh', 93, 100, 100, 70, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-08 15:11:28.188', '2026-05-08 15:11:28.188', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Yeah, sure. Let me quickly overview about me. I am Chindu Maru. I''ve been doing like a job with tech development for about like eight years now across like few different industries which have been cool, honestly. So right now I''m working at Comcast in Philly as a senior Java developer. I''ve been there like since October 2025, and you know, we are building like cloud native applications using Java 11, Python Spring Boot, and all running like on AWS. Like there is a pretty intense because so we are dealing with super high volume traffic, so event-driven architecture is kind of necessary. And we use Kafka heavily for processing between services. I''ve been working a lot of AWS Lambdas and also with DynamoDB and also step functions and also basically serverless and distributed system stuff. And also working with ELK stands for like a centralized logging, which has been like a lifesaver for instance. Before Comcast, I was like at the TD Bank in Mount Laurel for like about a year, like from September 2024 to September 2025. And then like I have been interested being dealing with the actual backlag transactions and also building spring boot microservices for like financial workflows and security as old spring security that also, we also use like AWS cases for like real-time event streaming and also built like some graphQL to reduce overfetching compared to REST. And which has been before that, I have been before that I have been like worked with wind supply in Dayton, Ohio, and also that has also has been retail supply chain and ASG that was my with season in Connecticut. That''s been like helped me like really about like new domains. Tech stack wise, my core tech stack is like Java, spring boot, and Hibernate and Spring Boot frameworks, and also JPA and also have a good with Angular and React frameworks and also with GraphQL and REST APIs. This is like an high level overview about me and I also did my master''s in computer science in New York in finished in like May 2023 and then later joined with supply.","Yeah, sure. Coming to uh let me clarify like uh we are like not heavily using Angular, but in my current project, like uh we are more into React.js, Python on the front-end side. Where do we like a UI work and honestly a lot of like I''m doing backend focus, Java, Spring Boot, AWS services, Kafka, that are a thing. And I also have like solid Angular experience from my previous roles, especially like Edwin supply. So Edwin Supply, I was building full Angular applications using TypeScript and material UI. Like we had like some of the internal dashboards for like supply chain management and inventory tracking and Angular component-based architecture was really nice for that because you could like uh build reusable UI components and two-way data binding and like forms really clean to work with. And honestly, I''ve been like more like a uh Angular person these days and React also. I''ve been more recently at TD Bank and built React.js UIs and Redux for state management and React Router for navigations. And Angular is catered like the concepts translate pretty well between two frameworks, you know, component lifecycle, state management, routing, different syntactics and patterns. So yeah. So definitely I have a good understanding and experience working with Angular. Yeah.","Yes, so, yeah, coming to RxJS, like especially uh super heavy in like production, so you know, like concept of reactive programming and observabilities, but uh done with RxJS like in Angular projects, anything like that. So I have worked with reactive patterns and different concepts like, you know, like at Comcast and TD Bank. I''ve been driving like a event-driven architecture with Kafka and AWS services, which has been like a conceptually similar. And you are dealing with like streams of data and you know, like a synchronous processing and handling back pressure and so that mindset of reactive programming. Um And also using to rather than imperative control flow, that is definitely comfortable with. Uh So I am very familiar with like reactive and event-driven patterns on the back end with Kafka and Kinesis, and I also built a streaming UIs. If RxJS is something you guys have lead, you have been conforming like a I can pick and stream like a big part of the front end full stack."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmox5gcso00bln20k8h01c1u8', 'cmox52b4h00b8n20kzbt5620w', 93, 100, 100, 70, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-08 16:48:51.008', '2026-05-08 16:48:51.008', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello.","Yeah, sure. So this is Sandhya. I''m a .NET developer, Senior .NET Full Stack Developer with 10 years of experience. Mostly I work with the, you know, the scalable applications like applications using Microsoft stack, primarily with the C Sharp, ASP.NET Core, .NET 6.7 blanks, Web APIs, and SQL Server. So over the years, I have worked across the full servers software development life cycles from requirement gathering, architecture discussions for the implementation, deployment, and even the production support. In my recent roles, I have focused mainly on the backend and also the cloud-based systems designing, RESTful APIs, improving the application, application performance, and the, you know, the modernization legacy applications into the microservices-oriented architectures. So I have been worked extensively with the Azure services, Docker, CI/CD pipelines, and also the automated testing to improve the reliability and also the deployment speed. Well, like what aligns with this role well with mine is not just a technical stack, but also as a leadership aspect. I have mentioned just, you know, the junior developers participated in the code reviews, collaborated closely with the, you know, the product and also the DevOps team and helped driving engineering best practices around the clean architecture, maintainability, and scalability. I''m especially interested in this opportunity because it seems to combine, you know, the hands-on engineering with ownership and also the collaboration, which, you know, where I can consistently deliver the most value.","So, well, when it comes to the design, you know, the migrated microservices architecture, the original system had come a difficulty to scale and maintain. So a single deployment impact for the, you know, the entire application, the release cycles were slow and the performance was bottleneck in one of the module affected, you know, unrelated functionality. So the goal is here is to improve the scalability, deployment independence, and the overall the system relevance. So I was the one of the senior developers responsible for the architecture and the migration strategies. So we started by identifying the bounded context within the, you know, the monolithic things like customer management and the order processing and the inventory notifications and also the balance. So from there, we gradually extracted those domains into, you know, the independent ASP.NET core microservices. Technically, the stack included having ASP.NET Core Web APIs, .NET 6, Azure Kubernetes services, and also the Azure services, Azure Service Bus for asynchronous messaging, and also SQL Server and Cosmo DB depending on the service needed, and also Docker for containerization and Azure DevOps for CI/CD pipeline. So in this, one of the key challenges that I was handling is like, you know, the communication between the services while avoiding the tight coupling. For synchronization operations, like we use the REST APIs, but for the workflows, like, you know, the order fulfillment or even the notifications, we implemented the event driving, you know, the communication throughout the Azure Service Bus. That helped mainly to improve the resilience and also reduce the direct service dependencies."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmoyjuwnu00ccn20kmpzlydi4', 'cmox7l1ym00bpn20ke6ekwity', 76, 76, 65, 94, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-09 16:19:50.818', '2026-05-09 16:20:00.088', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello.","Yes, can you hear me?","Okay. How are you?"]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmp4io87d00dwn20kls93llp2', 'cmp4i8u1400dgn20ktsium0t7', 95, 100, 100, 78, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-13 20:33:16.681', '2026-05-13 20:33:16.681', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hi, can you hear me? Hello? Hello?","Sure, my name is Puneet, and I am a Senior Data Engineer with AI and ML experience working with Generative AIs. And I have worked over like a decade of experience handling building large-scale data systems on AI-enabled data platforms across financial, healthcare, and retail sectors. My most recent client is with SMBC Mitsubishi Corporation Banking, where I have worked in designing cloud-native data pipelines, processing more than over 4 terabytes of data daily using AWS Glue, Snowflake, and Step Functions. And I have also been familiar with MuleSoft as well as along with the working procedures and architecture of the process. Alongside the traditional data engineering, I have been heavily involved in building AI-driven systems using AWS Bedrock and LangChain, and as well as including controversial and conventional data interfaces as well, where I automate decision pipelines. And I also specialize in building both batch and as well as real-time data pipelines, implementing strong data pipeline and data quality frameworks using the great expectations and designing scalable, designing the scalable architectures that supports, you know, the downstream architecture using APIs and as well as AI use cases. My most recent focus shifted towards generative AI frameworks in AI systems, building workflows, and as well as LLMs, where I interacted with structured data, APIs, and orchestrated layers to automate the complex business procedures. And I''m particularly interested in this role because the data engineering experiences along with my generative AI skills that I''ve been developing and working on designing systems, which exactly suits my specific field of interest where I can work and focus my work on my passion towards generative AI and my interest towards creating generative AI systems that I believe where my focus relies on in future. So I am pretty much sure this aligns with the current position of the AI and ML data engineer position that is offering by the organization.","Sure, I have built an auto-scaling computer using the ETL pipelines where I''ve used with Snowflake databases extensively on data warehouse layers in architectures, keeping things like data modeling using dimensions and data vault approaches, query optimizations using clusters and partitionings, integrations with databases from transformations, and also used secure data sharing and as well as role-based access. I have heavily worked on Snowflake in the backend and used AI use cases where I structured the data using the query dynamics, using the LLM generated, sorry."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmp4k96wt00f1n20knanwj48k', 'cmp4jvh4000ecn20kdvvh1a4a', 76, 100, 100, 94, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-13 21:17:34.397', '2026-05-22 03:45:42.388', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Yeah, sure. So I''m a senior full-stack Java developer with over 11 years of experience in designing, developing, and building some scalable enterprise applications using Java, Spring Boot, microservices, and RESTful APIs development. My recent work at Cigna has been focused on developing some Spring Boot microservices, event-driven systems with Apache Kafka, and integrating LLM and like a workflow and pre-trained models into enterprise applications. So before that, at City and MetLife, I worked on high-volume distributed systems, cloud-native deployments, and intelligent like search solutions using the Elasticsearch, Docker, Kubernetes, and AWS, or GPS cloud platforms I used with this. What aligns well with this is my hands-on experience in Java backend architecture, AI and ML integration, like even during the designs and building secure production-grade services in like agile environments. I also completed my master''s in information systems from the University of Memphis, which further strengthened my understanding on modern enterprise systems and data-driven application designs. Overall, my background is strongly aligned with this role because it combines core Java backend expertise with microservices, cloud, and AI and ML enterprise application development.","Yeah, yeah, sure. Like I have done like in current project I worked on healthcare platform where I designed and developed some SpringBoot-based microservices for a provider and claims-related workflows. My responsibility was to break a monolithic flow into a smaller services such as member data, claim processing, and providing the provider onboarding and expose them through RESTful APIs endpoints while using some Apache Kafka for real-time event streaming. And also I, like I used some Spring Cloud for service-to-service communication, Docker for containerization, and Kubernetes for AWS and for the Elastic Kubernetes service for deployment and scaling. I also implement security using OAuth2 and JSON Web Token for the secure API access and like leverage Elastic Search for interactive search capabilities on onboarding models. One good example was improving some claim lookup and provider''s workflow performance, like by what is by moving some legacy modules to microservices and optimizing the high-volume data access with MongoDB. So this helps like improve scalability deployment in like independence and also response time. So overall this project gave me strong hands-on experience in like design, secure, scalable, event-driven microservices using the Java and the Spring Boot in a production environment.","So I have done like in production, we use uh uh what is that? We use some uh monitoring and troubleshooting at multiple levels at Cigna was doing both microservices were like uh uh contact with the Docker and deployed in Kubernetes in the AWS. So I use the centralized logging Kubernetes uh all level checks and uh API level monitoring to quickly identify some failure like services. Like uh for troubleshooting, I typically start with application logs, so like a uh break off from message flow validations and development level checks to isolate like whether the issue is from the service, uh event pipeline or uh infrastructures. So since I also worked on uh ServiceNow uh ITSM and uh like uh CMDB, so integrated workflows like this incident tracking and root cause like a a follow-up were a part of uh part of the operational process as well. So one example was when I response time increased for uh like a uh claim lookup APIs and I analyzed MongoDB with query like a performance and optimized the data access layer, which like improved the API response time. So my approach is to combine proactive monitoring, log analysis, and uh infrastructure checks and performance tuning to keep uh like uh microservices stable in production."]', 'question-grade-hybrid', 'gpt-4.1-mini', 60, '[{"prompt":"Could you tell me a bit about your background and how it aligns with this role?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":7,"problem_solving":7,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":8},"strengths":["Strong background in Java backend development with over 11 years of experience.","Good coverage of microservices, RESTful APIs, and Spring Boot technologies.","Experience with cloud platforms (AWS, GCP), containerization (Docker, Kubernetes), and event-driven systems (Kafka).","Mentioned AI and ML integration including LLMs, reflecting alignment with role''s AI-driven architecture requirements.","Practical experience in distributed systems and agile development environments."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-0","weaknesses":["Did not explicitly mention experience with Neo4j or graph databases.","No direct reference to object-oriented design principles or specific design patterns like MVC or domain-driven design.","Limited mention of knowledge graph or semantic system projects and ontology-driven designs.","Communication could be clearer with fewer informal expressions (''like'') and more structured explanation."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":8,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate should summarize their 8–10+ years of professional experience focusing on backend development using Java and/or Node.js, emphasizing hands-on expertise in scalable system design with Spring Boot or Node frameworks. They should highlight specific experience designing and implementing RESTful APIs and microservices within distributed architectures, demonstrating a solid grasp of object-oriented design principles and common design patterns like MVC and domain-driven design. Additionally, they should reference practical exposure to graph databases such as Neo4j, including data modeling and Cypher querying, as well as involvement in knowledge graph or semantic system projects using ontology-driven designs. Experience with AI-driven architectures integrating large language models and semantic routing, along with CI/CD pipeline management, cloud platform familiarity (AWS/GCP), and container orchestrations, should also be noted. Finally, the candidate should illustrate their Agile/Scrum experience, cross-team collaboration skills, and any relevant examples tying their background directly to the responsibilities and technologies outlined in this role.","candidateAnswer":"Yeah, sure. So I''m a senior full-stack Java developer with over 11 years of experience in designing, developing, and building some scalable enterprise applications using Java, Spring Boot, microservices, and RESTful APIs development. My recent work at Cigna has been focused on developing some Spring Boot microservices, event-driven systems with Apache Kafka, and integrating LLM and like a workflow and pre-trained models into enterprise applications. So before that, at City and MetLife, I worked on high-volume distributed systems, cloud-native deployments, and intelligent like search solutions using the Elasticsearch, Docker, Kubernetes, and AWS, or GPS cloud platforms I used with this. What aligns well with this is my hands-on experience in Java backend architecture, AI and ML integration, like even during the designs and building secure production-grade services in like agile environments. I also completed my master''s in information systems from the University of Memphis, which further strengthened my understanding on modern enterprise systems and data-driven application designs. Overall, my background is strongly aligned with this role because it combines core Java backend expertise with microservices, cloud, and AI and ML enterprise application development.","missing_concepts":["Neo4j or graph database experience","Object-oriented design principles and design patterns","Knowledge graph or semantic system projects","Explicit Agile/Scrum process description"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate provides a solid overview of their technical background, emphasizing extensive Java backend development using Spring Boot and microservices, which strongly aligns with the role requirements. They demonstrate familiarity with cloud technologies and container orchestration and mention integration of AI/ML components like large language models. However, the answer falls short in detailing experience with Neo4j graph databases, object-oriented design principles, and specific design patterns that are key in this role. Additionally, the candidate could improve clarity and professionalism by avoiding filler words and providing a more structured answer. Overall, the candidate shows good technical understanding and relevant experience but misses some important role-specific technologies and concepts.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate shows strong Java backend and microservices expertise, relevant cloud and AI integration experience, and solid distributed system knowledge. While some specialized graph database and design pattern experience is missing, they appear capable of fulfilling the core technical demands of the role. The response demonstrates a good fit but with room for deeper knowledge in specific technol"},{"prompt":"Can you walk me through a specific project where you designed and developed a set of microservices using Java and Spring Boot?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":7,"problem_solving":8,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":8},"strengths":["Demonstrated experience designing and developing Java Spring Boot microservices with domain separation (member data, claim processing, provider onboarding).","Mentioned RESTful APIs and use of asynchronous event streaming with Apache Kafka for scalability and real-time data.","Used Spring Cloud for service communication and Docker/Kubernetes for containerization and deployment.","Implemented OAuth2 and JWT for security covers typical API protection mechanisms.","Discussed performance improvements through migration from legacy to microservices and database optimization."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-1","weaknesses":["Explanation was somewhat disjointed and occasionally unclear, with filler words reducing clarity.","Did not elaborate much on persistence frameworks (e.g. Spring Data JPA) explicitly or testing processes.","No mention of centralized configuration management like Spring Cloud Config.","Limited detail on asynchronous communication patterns beyond mentioning Kafka.","Missing explicit discussion on integration or unit testing and CI/CD pipeline."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":8,"expectedAnswer":"In a recent project, I led the design and development of a microservices-based order management system using Java and Spring Boot. I decomposed the monolithic application into domain-driven services such as Order, Inventory, and Payment, each exposing RESTful APIs with Spring MVC. I applied object-oriented design principles and used Spring Data JPA for persistence. The services communicated asynchronously via message brokers to ensure loose coupling and scalability. I implemented centralized configuration with Spring Cloud Config and employed containerization using Docker for deployment. The system was secured using OAuth 2.0 and tested with integration and unit tests within a CI/CD pipeline.","candidateAnswer":"Yeah, yeah, sure. Like I have done like in current project I worked on healthcare platform where I designed and developed some SpringBoot-based microservices for a provider and claims-related workflows. My responsibility was to break a monolithic flow into a smaller services such as member data, claim processing, and providing the provider onboarding and expose them through RESTful APIs endpoints while using some Apache Kafka for real-time event streaming. And also I, like I used some Spring Cloud for service-to-service communication, Docker for containerization, and Kubernetes for AWS and for the Elastic Kubernetes service for deployment and scaling. I also implement security using OAuth2 and JSON Web Token for the secure API access and like leverage Elastic Search for interactive search capabilities on onboarding models. One good example was improving some claim lookup and provider''s workflow performance, like by what is by moving some legacy modules to microservices and optimizing the high-volume data access with MongoDB. So this helps like improve scalability deployment in like independence and also response time. So overall this project gave me strong hands-on experience in like design, secure, scalable, event-driven microservices using the Java and the Spring Boot in a production environment.","missing_concepts":["Use of Spring Data JPA or equivalent ORM framework for persistence","Centralized configuration management (e.g. Spring Cloud Config)","Explicit mention of testing strategies (unit, integration) and CI/CD pipeline integration","More structured explanation of asynchronous communication design (message brokers, event-driven)","Clear description of domain-driven design principles"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrated solid practical experience designing and developing microservices using Java and Spring Boot in a meaningful healthcare domain. They correctly discussed domain decomposition, RESTful APIs, asynchronous communication with Kafka, containerization with Docker and Kubernetes, and secured APIs with OAuth2 and JWT. They also highlighted improvements in scalability and performance by migrating legacy components and optimizing data access, indicating good problem-solving. However, the answer was somewhat informal and occasionally repetitive, which affected communication clarity. There was a lack of explicit mention of some best practices like the use of Spring Data JPA, centralized configuration management, and testing approaches, which reduces completeness against the ideal technical depth expected. Overall, the candidate meets a solid intermediate level in microservices design and development with Java Spring Boot, with room for improved clarity and detail in some areas.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate has good hands-on experience designing and implementing Java Spring Boot microservices within a real project, particularly well-versed in event-driven architecture, containerization, and security. Communication could be clearer and more structured. More depth on testing, ORM/persistence frameworks, and configuration management would strengthen the overall mastery. Pass with moderate conf"},{"prompt":"How did you handle monitoring and troubleshooting these microservices in production?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":4,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Candidate demonstrated awareness of centralized logging and monitoring at multiple levels including Kubernetes and API level.","Mentioned practical troubleshooting starting with application logs and isolating issues between service, pipeline, or infrastructure.","Integration with ITSM tools for incident tracking showed operational process understanding.","Provided a relevant example involving performance tuning of MongoDB queries to improve API response time."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-2","weaknesses":["Answer was vague, disorganized, and lacked structure, reducing clarity and professionalism.","Failed to mention standard tools and concepts from the ideal answer such as ELK Stack, Prometheus, Grafana, distributed tracing (Jaeger/Zipkin), circuit breakers, and health checks.","Did not clearly explain metrics collection or alerting mechanisms for proactive detection.","Communication was hindered by hesitation and filler words, making understanding difficult."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"To monitor and troubleshoot microservices in production, I implemented centralized logging using tools like ELK Stack or Splunk, and set up distributed tracing (e.g., with Jaeger or Zipkin) to track requests across services. I used metrics collection and alerting via Prometheus and Grafana to detect anomalies and performance bottlenecks. For troubleshooting, I analyzed logs and trace data to identify failures or latency causes, and employed automated health checks with circuit breakers to ensure system resiliency. This observability setup enabled quick root cause analysis and reduced mean time to recovery (MTTR).","candidateAnswer":"So I have done like in production, we use uh uh what is that? We use some uh monitoring and troubleshooting at multiple levels at Cigna was doing both microservices were like uh uh contact with the Docker and deployed in Kubernetes in the AWS. So I use the centralized logging Kubernetes uh all level checks and uh API level monitoring to quickly identify some failure like services. Like uh for troubleshooting, I typically start with application logs, so like a uh break off from message flow validations and development level checks to isolate like whether the issue is from the service, uh event pipeline or uh infrastructures. So since I also worked on uh ServiceNow uh ITSM and uh like uh CMDB, so integrated workflows like this incident tracking and root cause like a a follow-up were a part of uh part of the operational process as well. So one example was when I response time increased for uh like a uh claim lookup APIs and I analyzed MongoDB with query like a performance and optimized the data access layer, which like improved the API response time. So my approach is to combine proactive monitoring, log analysis, and uh infrastructure checks and performance tuning to keep uh like uh microservices stable in production.","missing_concepts":["Centralized logging tools like ELK Stack or Splunk","Distributed tracing solutions (e.g., Jaeger, Zipkin)","Metrics collection and alerting (Prometheus, Grafana)","Automated health checks and circuit breakers","Explicit description of how monitoring reduces MTTR"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate shows a basic understanding of monitoring and troubleshooting microservices with mention of centralized logging, Kubernetes level checks, and real use of ITSM tools for incident management. They also demonstrated problem-solving ability with an example of MongoDB query optimization. However, the response lacks technical depth and completeness, missing key industry tools and best practices for microservices observabili