ked down properly. And before that, I have been working with Wind Supply in Ohio and before like I assume worked with AA and Connecticab where I worked as an insurance applications. And I also done like masters in computer science from Marist College in New York, which I was finished in 2023 and was working in like India at like Eval solutions for about four and a half years, like code, Java development, Spring boot framework. And you are looking for someone with like a strong backend with Java and spring boot microservices and the AI, ML integration is a piece of interesting to me because I''ve been working with Comcast like integrated OpenAI LLM APIs for like a log summarization and incident rise. And I built like an internal RAG-based application during hackathon like using fast API and quadrant and Olama. So that idea of bringing like AI into enterprise Java applications is something I genuinely excited about. You know, I''ve been working with AWS like extensively, EC2, Lambda and S3 and cloud formation. And yeah, I think my background is distributed systems, microservices, cloud infrastructure, and I''m also like AI, ML exposure would be pretty good fit for this what I''m looking for.","Yes, sure. Coming to that, I''ve been working with RESTful APIs and microservices pretty much throughout my career, but I would say the most intensive experience that was been like at Comcast and TD Bank where we are dealing with like a highly like a high volume distributed systems. So at Comcast, we are building like cloud native applications with Spring Boot, microservices, and running on AWS and like architecture is like fully distributed. So I have the services communicating via REST APIs and also event-driven patterns with like a Kafka and AWS Lambda. And one thing I have been worked was like designing RESTful APIs for, you know, like backend services that handle like a customer data workflows. And we have like made sure this API is where like scalable and reliable because we are talking about millions of requests per day, right? So I''m designing a RESTful API always starts with like a resource model that are like entities with operations to need and I follow RESTful principles pretty strictly, get for reads, post for creates, put for updates, deletes for deletes and proper HTTP codes, you know, like 200 for success, 400 for not found, 500 for server errors. At Comcast, I also implemented standard exceptional handling across our microservices because before it kind of inconsistent and debugging was nightmare. So now every service returns like a consistent error response structure with error codes, message and correlation ID for tracing. And GraphQL actually for like some of these use cases, which let like client requests exactly filled I needed like a query instead of multiple REST calls and the page load like improved noticeably in terms of distributed architecture. One of the big things I''ve been learned and Is that like just you cannot just treat microservices like a monolith. You have like a service discovery and distributed tracing and centralized logging at the infrastructure. At the Comcast, I set up like end to end like request correlations using Java MDC and Python structured logging. So every request like a unique correlation ID which flows through the services and we can track by using like ELK stack dashboards that has been a huge for troubleshooting production issues. You can exactly know which request failed and which downstream services like called like and it also worked with event driven patterns. Like at Comcast, we use like Kafka heavily for like asynchronous processing and these have been like services we can consume and do their own processing."]', 'heuristic', null, null, null), ('cmp4ny7dg00gfn20krdw5vrxf', 'cmp4nivx500g2n20kqqslbssp', 67, 100, 100, 70, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-13 23:01:00.241', '2026-05-22 16:48:09.784', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["I can''t hear you. Can you please repeat?","I started off with my master''s in machine learning and I''ve been working as a data engineer, as an AI engineer for almost like 10 plus years in the IT industry. And during this experience, I have extensively worked on setting up the building of the ETL pipelines from scratch and identifying the data models, working with the stakeholders of the product team, and also working with various team members such as data scientists, data analysts, or in case of this like cross-application team members as well. And while doing so, I was able to work on various tools on warehouses such as like Snowflake, GCP, AWS, and all. And my programming expertise relies on specifically on Python and Java. And I have like very, I would consider myself as an expert in SQL. And in my recent projects, I have like extensively utilized different AI or like LLM models in our project to optimize a few approaches like that we are considering in our current pipelines and also working or utilizing these same models and models to extensively enhance the golden data set that we are trying to prepare.","Sure. So as I explained, right, I have used Python in Snowflake across my career. And Python is like I have used in multiple stages. So to talk about statistically, how do we set up an ETL pipeline with Python in Snowflake here is, so firstly, I will try to identify the sources that are application source teams, which are managing the ETL pipeline. And I would identify what are the list of sources and I will create a data model out of each table and then have these source team respective tables, which will be considered as a raw layer, which will be pulled in using the Python scripts. For example, if it is a ODBC connection Oracle database, we can use ODBC or any JDBC for SQL server already has a connection. And using SQLAlchemy or Pyspark copy tool. And we can connect to these databases. And we can set up these ETL triggers on these databases to identify the changes that are happening on these individual tables as well. That was old technique and the new technique we can use, it is golden gate to retrieve the CSV files or else the Postgres SQL has what do we say, the VAL archive and also SQL server has this SSIS and SSIS, which will generate these CSV files as we need as a changes. Maybe we can consume this and then land them in the Snowflake staging area. So in the Snowflake staging area, we can create this external table using Python ETL Python script excels. And again, which will execute, we can have these SQL queries that we want to create, which will do the transformation. So in the ETL pipeline, the major concentration that I would give is having the medullin architecture, which is like very optimized one using bronze, silver, gold, which can be acting as like a raw transform then and the final golden data set. So in this way, like I would set up this Python script along with the Snowflake to continuously transform the data and then filter the data as needed and by creating these multiple SQL queries and then creating these temporary tables at runtime on the Snowflake instance using Python, I will be able to do this. And for orchestration, I can utilize Airflow to execute these Python scripts on which will do the execution of the Snowflake, this ETL pipeline flow."]', 'question-grade-hybrid', 'gpt-4.1-mini', 50, '[{"prompt":"Can you start by telling me about your background and how it maps to this role?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":6,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Demonstrated extensive experience with ETL pipelines and data modeling.","Experience with data warehouses including Snowflake, GCP, and AWS.","Strong programming skills in Python, SQL, and Java.","Hands-on exposure to AI and LLM models for pipeline optimization and data preparation."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-0","weaknesses":["Did not mention specific tools like Airflow, MuleSoft, or Salesforce integration.","Lacked explicit discussion about data quality, monitoring, or workflow automation.","No mention of use of LangChain, LangGraph, or specific AI/ML agentic frameworks.","Some statements were somewhat vague without concrete examples."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":7,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate would describe their experience as a senior data engineer with a focus on AI/ML applications, highlighting hands-on expertise in building and managing ETL pipelines using tools like Informatica and Python. They would emphasize their proficiency with data architectures including data warehousing and modeling, particularly in Snowflake and relational databases, and their experience in orchestrating workflows with Airflow and MuleSoft. The candidate should link their practical work integrating Salesforce platforms and managing data egress to CRMs, demonstrating understanding of Salesforce data models and flows.\\n\\nAdditionally, they would discuss their experience with AI/ML engineering, including working with LLM APIs such as OpenAI or Anthropic, prompt engineering, and building agentic frameworks with LangChain or LangGraph. They should provide examples of designing scalable real-time and batch data pipelines, employing dbt for data transformation, and working with vector databases and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipelines.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Several years of data engineering with ETL and data architecture\\n- Integration of Salesforce systems and CRM data egress\\n- Advanced SQL and Python scripting for custom workflows\\n- Experience with Airflow, MuleSoft, and cloud platforms (AWS/GCP)\\n- Development and deployment of AI-driven agentic capabilities using LLMs and related frameworks\\n- Practical knowledge of data quality and monitoring best practices\\n- Systems thinking and workflow automation relevant to agentic systems","candidateAnswer":"I started off with my master''s in machine learning and I''ve been working as a data engineer, as an AI engineer for almost like 10 plus years in the IT industry. And during this experience, I have extensively worked on setting up the building of the ETL pipelines from scratch and identifying the data models, working with the stakeholders of the product team, and also working with various team members such as data scientists, data analysts, or in case of this like cross-application team members as well. And while doing so, I was able to work on various tools on warehouses such as like Snowflake, GCP, AWS, and all. And my programming expertise relies on specifically on Python and Java. And I have like very, I would consider myself as an expert in SQL. And in my recent projects, I have like extensively utilized different AI or like LLM models in our project to optimize a few approaches like that we are considering in our current pipelines and also working or utilizing these same models and models to extensively enhance the golden data set that we are trying to prepare.","missing_concepts":["Salesforce platforms and CRM data egress integration.","Airflow and MuleSoft for orchestration.","LLM API specifics such as OpenAI or Anthropic.","Use of dbt for data transformations.","Vector databases and RAG pipelines.","Systems thinking and workflow automation relevant to agentic systems."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrated solid technical knowledge in building ETL pipelines, data warehousing, and AI/ML model usage, aligning well with the core data engineering aspects of the role. Their experience with Python and SQL is a definite strength. However, the answer lacked depth in key areas related to this role such as integration with Salesforce systems, orchestration tools like Airflow or MuleSoft, and advanced AI/ML frameworks like LangChain. The explanation was somewhat general and missed linking specific technologies and concrete examples that demonstrate comprehensive skills mapping to the role''s demands. Communication was clear but could benefit from more structured detail highlighting relevant projects and systems thinking approaches. Overall, a good foundation but with notable gaps needing addressing to fully meet the ideal profile.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate shows strong foundational skills in data engineering and some AI/ML experience, particularly with ETL pipelines and cloud data warehouses. They communicate well and have relevant technical capabilities; however, they do not sufficiently cover critical elements such as Salesforce integration, orchestration tools, and specific AI agentic frameworks. Candidate passes but would require furth"},{"prompt":"Can you tell me about a specific example where you built a complex ETL pipeline from scratch using Python and Snowflake?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":6,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":5,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Mentions identifying data sources and data modeling","References using Python with Snowflake for ETL processes","Acknowledges using frameworks and tools like SQLAlchemy, PySpark, Airflow","Discusses staged architecture with bronze, silver, gold layers","Considers incremental loads and triggers for change data capture"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-1","weaknesses":["Answer is somewhat vague and lacks a clear, concrete example","Does not clearly describe the pipeline design and integration steps end-to-end","Use of some terminology is confusing or inaccurately presented (e.g., ''golden gate'' context unclear)","Limited explanation on how transformations and loading are implemented specifically","Lacks coverage of error handling, idempotency, monitoring, and performance optimizations"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"An ideal answer would describe designing and implementing a scalable ETL pipeline starting from data ingestion, transformation, and loading into Snowflake using Python. A strong candidate should highlight requirements gathering, data source integration (e.g., third-party APIs, Salesforce data), and data modeling to fit Snowflake’s architecture. They should mention using Python libraries to extract data, performing complex transformations with pandas or SQLAlchemy, and loading data efficiently into Snowflake with batch and incremental load patterns, ensuring idempotency and error handling. They would also cover pipeline orchestration using Airflow or similar, data quality checks, and performance considerations like query optimization and parallelism. Finally, the candidate might discuss monitoring and alerting to maintain pipeline health.","candidateAnswer":"Sure. So as I explained, right, I have used Python in Snowflake across my career. And Python is like I have used in multiple stages. So to talk about statistically, how do we set up an ETL pipeline with Python in Snowflake here is, so firstly, I will try to identify the sources that are application source teams, which are managing the ETL pipeline. And I would identify what are the list of sources and I will create a data model out of each table and then have these source team respective tables, which will be considered as a raw layer, which will be pulled in using the Python scripts. For example, if it is a ODBC connection Oracle database, we can use ODBC or any JDBC for SQL server already has a connection. And using SQLAlchemy or Pyspark copy tool. And we can connect to these databases. And we can set up these ETL triggers on these databases to identify the changes that are happening on these individual tables as well. That was old technique and the new technique we can use, it is golden gate to retrieve the CSV files or else the Postgres SQL has what do we say, the VAL archive and also SQL server has this SSIS and SSIS, which will generate these CSV files as we need as a changes. Maybe we can consume this and then land them in the Snowflake staging area. So in the Snowflake staging area, we can create this external table using Python ETL Python script excels. And again, which will execute, we can have these SQL queries that we want to create, which will do the transformation. So in the ETL pipeline, the major concentration that I would give is having the medullin architecture, which is like very optimized one using bronze, silver, gold, which can be acting as like a raw transform then and the final golden data set. So in this way, like I would set up this Python script along with the Snowflake to continuously transform the data and then filter the data as needed and by creating these multiple SQL queries and then creating these temporary tables at runtime on the Snowflake instance using Python, I will be able to do this. And for orchestration, I can utilize Airflow to execute these Python scripts on which will do the execution of the Snowflake, this ETL pipeline flow.","missing_concepts":["Detailed example of data ingestion from specific sources with Python code or libraries","Explicit description of complex transformations using pandas or SQL","Loading data efficiently into Snowflake including batch/incremental load patterns","Error handling and idempotency strategies","Monitoring and alerting mechanisms for pipeline health","Performance considerations such as query optimization and parallelism"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate shows a reasonable understanding of ETL concepts and the use of Python and Snowflake together, including staging layers and orchestration with Airflow. However, the answer lacks a concrete, step-by-step example of building a pipeline from scratch, which was requested. The explanation is fragmented and sometimes inaccurate, with unclear or misused terminology. Important aspects like error handling, idempotency, data quality checks, and monitoring were not addressed. The response would be stronger if it included a specific use case, described how data is ingested and transformed in detail, and covered performance and operational best practices. Communication could be improved to present a clear and structured response.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate demonstrates familiarity with key components involved in ETL pipelines using Python and Snowflake and mentions relevant tools and architectural patterns. However, the explanation is incomplete, lacks depth in key areas, and is somewhat unclear. There is insufficient concrete evidence of hands-on experience building a complete complex ETL pipeline from scratch. Overall, while the cand"},{"prompt":"How did you handle any performance optimizations in that pipeline?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":5,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Mentions important optimization techniques like partitioning and clustering on Snowflake tables","Understands the importance of join performance and filtering (partition pruning)","Acknowledges pipeline fault tolerance via recreating data and handling failures"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-2","weaknesses":["Answer is somewhat vague and lacks structured explanation of steps taken","Does not mention profiling or identifying bottlenecks explicitly","No concrete examples of parallel processing or Airflow DAG optimization","Incremental data loading not clearly discussed","Communication is somewhat disorganized and repetitive"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"To optimize performance in the pipeline, I first conducted profiling to identify bottlenecks in data ingestion and transformation steps. I implemented parallel processing for batch operations using Python multiprocessing and leveraged Snowflake''s clustering keys and partitioning to speed up query times. I also optimized Airflow DAG scheduling and task dependencies to reduce idle time and improve throughput. Additionally, I employed incremental data loading where possible to minimize data volume processed each run. Monitoring with custom metrics and alerts ensured performance remained within SLAs.","candidateAnswer":"So let''s say how do we handle performance of this? So here there could be various reasons. So one, we need to understand like different kinds of joins that we are trying to do, and we need to ensure if the table is created, is it partitioned or ordered by a specific key column using like something like a cluster by column, which will ensure that the data is partitioned and if we have to do any where clauses or if we have to do any joins with another table using that column, it will be efficient join. So in this way, like we can do some optimization and on the Snowflake side, if we can do this create table, I would say while creating the pipeline, I would ensure that we can do a backtrace step, meaning we can set up the Snowflake tables in such a way that I mean like the pipeline in such a way that even if there is a failure, like we can just recreate the data again using different like the slowly changing dimension table. And with respect to optimization, those were like some techniques which I explained earlier, like joins and also the partition pruning and enhanced cluster warehousing, like the Snowflake cluster, virtual warehouse setup. In this way, we can ensure that the whole pipeline is running in an optimized way.","missing_concepts":["Profiling and bottleneck identification","Parallel processing for batch operations","Airflow DAG scheduling and task dependency optimization","Incremental data loading strategies","Monitoring with custom metrics and alerts"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrated a decent understanding of Snowflake-specific optimizations such as clustering keys and partitioning, which are relevant for join and query performance. They also touched on fault tolerance by mentioning the ability to recreate data in case of pipeline failure, showing some grasp on robustness. However, the answer lacked clarity and coherence, making it harder to follow the thought process. Crucial steps such as profiling to find bottlenecks, use of parallel processing, incremental loading techniques, and Airflow DAG optimization were missing. The response also didn''t clearly address monitoring or continuous performance management. Overall, the candidate showed partial knowledge but did not fully meet expectations for completeness and communication clarity.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate provided some relevant ideas around Snowflake optimizations but failed to comprehensively explain their approach to pipeline performance improvements. While technically correct in parts, the answer was vague and incomplete, and communication was somewhat unclear. More detail on profiling, parallelism, incremental loading, and orchestration optimizations would be needed to pass."},{"prompt":"How have you worked with LLM APIs and agentic frameworks like LangChain in your recent projects?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":7,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":4,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Demonstrated practical use of LLMs to improve testing coverage in a complex repo environment","Used LangChain to assign multiple roles to LLMs for data creation and validation","Described integration of cloud services and Python for LLM usage"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-3","weaknesses":["Lack of clear and structured explanation about how LangChain was used agentically to decompose tasks","Unclear technical details on the LLM API integration and no mention of specific APIs like OpenAI or Anthropic","Poor communication clarity with incomplete sentences and vague descriptions","No explicit mention of handling prompt management, context or orchestration within LangChain","Limited mention of relevant data engineering workflows like ETL or integration with Salesforce CRM"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"I have integrated LLM APIs such as OpenAI and Anthropic into data workflows to enhance automated decision-making and natural language understanding. Using LangChain, I built agentic pipelines that decompose complex tasks into manageable sub-tasks executed by autonomous agents, improving efficiency in data ingestion and processing. For example, I developed multi-step ETL workflows where LangChain orchestrated calls to LLMs for prompt engineering, data enrichment, and real-time response generation. This involved customizing Python modules to interact with the APIs, managing prompt context, and handling agentic task coordination, ensuring seamless integration with existing data pipelines and downstream Salesforce CRM systems.","candidateAnswer":"Yeah, so coming to LLM models usage in my current work. So we had this scenario where we have the five-part code set up. We had a huge repository where we kind of a monolithic repository where we have like multiple teams contributing to it on different, let''s say, strategies. That''s what we call. And for each strategy and when the team is enhancing or the code changes, we were covering the unit test cases and we had an issue with where the coverage is getting dropped continuously day by day. So using LLM, we have introduced LLM models. We integrated a cloud in our model, I mean, in our document, like the cloud and all. And we created an environment where we will install cloud and we will provide this repository. And whenever it will, whenever the testing threshold, if it is dropping less than 80%, it will identify the biggest changes that it can bring, I mean, add the test cases to enhance it up to and then add more test cases to it. That is one way where we optimized our current flow. And in the code itself, we have utilized LLM to identify, for example, if we have any patient information, for example, if there are any null values, like what could be the probable value, for example, like some zip code, like what could be the values that we can populate and some scenarios we can identify. In this way, I have worked with like extensively with cloud to integrate using Python and Langchain. So using Langchain, we were able to establish like multiple roles for these models, which will act as one will act as the data creator and one will act as the another model will act as the data validator. In this way, whatever the data is created by LLM as in an ad hoc way also, we are just ensuring that this is created in a proper way and there is no issue going forward.","missing_concepts":["Explicit integration details of LLM APIs (OpenAI, Anthropic)","Agentic decomposition of tasks and orchestration with LangChain","Handling prompt engineering and context management","Workflow examples with multi-step ETL processes and real-time data enrichment","Integration with downstream business platforms like Salesforce"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate showed some practical usage of LLMs and LangChain, particularly in improving test coverage and assigning roles for data creation and validation, which aligns somewhat with agentic concepts. However, the explanation lacked clarity and technical precision. They did not provide specific details on which LLM APIs were used or how LangChain orchestrated complex workflows. The answer was vague about prompt context handling, multi-step task decomposition, or integration into broader data pipelines and business systems. Communication was hampered by unclear sentence structures and incomplete descriptions. Overall, the answer indicates partial understanding but misses critical agentic framework and data engineering integration details expected at this level.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate has some exposure to using LLMs and LangChain in a practical environment but struggles to clearly articulate the technical implementation and agentic framework benefits. Their understanding appears partial and somewhat disorganized, limiting insight into their problem-solving skills and completeness. More structured and detailed responses are needed to demonstrate strong competence f"},{"prompt":"How did you manage integrating LangChain with your existing data pipelines?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":0,"completeness":0,"problem_solving":0,"communication_clarity":0,"technical_correctness":0},"strengths":[],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-4","weaknesses":["No answer provided to evaluate technical skills or knowledge.","No demonstration of understanding of integration or related technologies."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":0,"expectedAnswer":"Integrating LangChain with existing data pipelines involved creating modular Python components that wrapped LangChain''s agentic capabilities around established ETL workflows. I ensured smooth data flow by connecting LangChain''s semantic search and LLM interaction layers with pipeline output datasets stored in Snowflake and downstream Salesforce CRM ingestion points. This required orchestrating LangChain processes via Airflow, handling authentication securely for LLM API calls, and designing retry and error-handling mechanisms to maintain pipeline reliability. I also collaborated closely with stakeholders to align prompt engineering and data schema expectations for seamless agentic data consumption.","candidateAnswer":"","missing_concepts":["Modular Python components for LangChain integration","ETL workflow integration","Data flow management with LangChain semantic search and LLM interaction","Use of Snowflake and Salesforce CRM in pipeline","Orchestration with Airflow","Authentication and error handling for LLM API","Collaboration for prompt engineering and data schema alignment"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate did not provide any answer to the question regarding integration of LangChain with existing data pipelines. As a result, no evaluation could be made on their technical knowledge, problem-solving approach, or communication skills. Integrating LangChain typically requires understanding of ETL processes, LLM APIs, data orchestration tools, security practices, and collaboration with stakeholders, none of which were addressed or mentioned here. Without a response, the candidate fails to demonstrate any capability in this area.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate did not answer the integration question, providing no insight into their technical abilities or understanding of LangChain and data pipeline integration. This lack of response leads to a fail for this critical competency assessment."}]'), ('cmp6wi16h000jnx0kkmhqjk5n', 'cmp6w29j20003nx0kycacd354', 72, 100, 100, 78, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 12:35:54.617', '2026-05-22 16:51:36.344', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello.","Hi David, this is Ashmika and I''ve been working in the software industry for more than 15 years now and I have worked in various domains like insurance, banking, finance.","And healthcare. And also, I have done a lot of work on Java, Springboard, and other server-side technologies, along with a good amount of experience working in SQL, database, and as well as front-end technologies like React, Angular. And that''s why I think I''m a good fit for this project, plus this project also needs somebody who can mentor the other team members, along with that, also take responsibilities, multiple responsibilities. So I have already done that in a lot of my earlier projects, and that''s why I find myself fit for this."]', 'question-grade-hybrid', 'gpt-4.1-mini', 56, '[{"prompt":"Could you begin by telling me a bit about your background and how it maps to this Software Engineer role?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":7,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Candidate has 15 years of software industry experience","Experience with Java and Spring Boot mentioned","Familiarity with front-end technologies like React and Angular","Mentoring and leadership experience highlighted"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-0","weaknesses":["No mention of specific backend architectural experience such as microservices or RESTful APIs","No explicit experience shared with graph technologies like Neo4j or AI/LLM related architectures","No mention of Agile/Scrum, CI/CD, cloud platforms, or containerization","The answer is somewhat vague about technical depth and scalability aspects"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate should describe their 8–10+ years of experience in backend development, particularly with Java and/or Node.js, emphasizing their expertise in building scalable systems using Spring Boot or Node frameworks. They should mention hands-on experience designing and developing RESTful APIs and microservices in distributed architectures, demonstrating a solid grasp of object-oriented design principles and design patterns such as MVC or domain-driven design. Additionally, the ideal answer includes experience with graph technologies like Neo4j, familiarity with graph data modeling and query languages like Cypher, and knowledge of AI-driven architectures involving semantic routing or LLM integration. Highlighting practical involvement in Agile/Scrum teams, CI/CD pipelines, cloud platforms (AWS/GCP), and containerized deployments reinforces the candidate''s fit. The candidate might conclude by linking how these skills and experiences directly enable them to contribute effectively to this Software Engineer role.","candidateAnswer":"Hi David, this is Ashmika and I''ve been working in the software industry for more than 15 years now and I have worked in various domains like insurance, banking, finance. And healthcare. And also, I have done a lot of work on Java, Springboard, and other server-side technologies, along with a good amount of experience working in SQL, database, and as well as front-end technologies like React, Angular. And that''s why I think I''m a good fit for this project, plus this project also needs somebody who can mentor the other team members, along with that, also take responsibilities, multiple responsibilities. So I have already done that in a lot of my earlier projects, and that''s why I find myself fit for this.","missing_concepts":["Microservices architecture","RESTful API design and implementation","Object-oriented design principles and design patterns","Graph database experience (e.g., Neo4j, Cypher)","AI-driven architecture with semantic routing or LLM integration","Agile/Scrum methodology, CI/CD pipelines","Cloud platforms (AWS, GCP) and container deployment"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate provides a broad overview of their background, highlighting extensive experience in the software industry, particularly with Java and Spring Boot, as well as front-end technologies. They also mention mentoring and handling multiple responsibilities, which is positive. However, the answer lacks key specifics about the backend development skills and architectural knowledge expected for this role, such as experience with microservices, REST APIs, graph databases, and AI-driven systems. Moreover, critical modern software engineering practices like Agile methodologies, CI/CD, and cloud platform experience are not addressed. The communication is clear but somewhat generic and incomplete in mapping skills directly to the role requirements. To improve, the candidate should provide more technical depth and explicitly relate their experience to the key technologies and practices relevant to the role.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate demonstrates significant software industry experience and familiarity with Java and some front-end technologies, along with leadership capabilities. However, they fall short in articulating their experience with backend system design, microservices, graph databases, AI integrations, and essential DevOps practices required for this role. Consequently, while technically correct in part"},{"prompt":"Can you walk me through your hands-on experience with building scalable systems using Java and Spring Boot?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":6,"problem_solving":7,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Mentions modular, stateless, and distributed architecture","Covers use of REST APIs with Spring Boot microservices","Highlights Docker, Kubernetes for deployment","References asynchronous communication using Kafka, RabbitMQ","Includes caching strategy using Redis"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-1","weaknesses":["Answer is somewhat generic and lacks concrete examples","No specific hands-on experience or project details provided","Limited mention of fault tolerance or connection pooling","No mention of security practices such as OAuth2 or JWT","Explanation is somewhat vague and repetitive"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":7,"expectedAnswer":"I have over 8 years of experience developing scalable backend systems using Java and Spring Boot. My work involved designing microservices architectures that support high concurrency and fault tolerance, deploying services on cloud platforms like AWS with containerization (Docker/Kubernetes), and implementing asynchronous processing with message brokers such as Kafka or RabbitMQ to handle load spikes efficiently. I routinely employ best practices such as proper exception handling, caching strategies (e.g., Redis), and connection pooling to optimize performance. Additionally, I design RESTful APIs adhering to standard conventions and secure them using OAuth2/JWT. For example, in a recent project, I refactored a monolith into modular Spring Boot microservices to improve scalability and maintainability while ensuring zero downtime during deployment through CI/CD pipelines.","candidateAnswer":"Yes, in order to build a scalable system using Java and Spring Boot, the most important thing is to make sure that we are interacting both the systems together and making sure that the Java and Spring Boot are both making the system much more scalable, reliable, and at the same time, making the system much more flexible so that any new component can be introduced at any point of time. So basically, I would design such a scalable system by keeping in mind the architecture should be modular, it should be stateless, also distributed architecture would be best, and like I would also concentrate on performance optimization. So the Spring Boot microservices can be exposed using REST API, that will make the system much more better in many ways. And at the application level, I will keep the services mostly stateless. That will help me in many ways, and I''ll use load balancers, and I''ll also use some kind of, like, for deployment purposes, I''ll use Docker, Kubernetes, and I''ll focus more on proper indexing of the system. At the same time, query optimization, all those things will make my system much more better. In terms of asynchronous communication, I would prefer using Kafka, RabbitMQ, or Spring batches. So that will be so helpful in making sure that the proper interaction between the components happens. For caching, I can go for using Redis in my system, and overall, I''ll build a scalable Java Spring system using microservices, and I''ll use stateless services, load balancing, caching, combination of all these and make my system much better.","missing_concepts":["Concrete hands-on experience with Java and Spring Boot projects","Implementation details like exception handling, connection pooling","Security measures such as OAuth2/JWT for APIs","CI/CD practices for zero downtime deployments","Specifics on microservices design challenges solved"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrates a good understanding of key concepts for building scalable systems using Java and Spring Boot, including microservices, stateless design, asynchronous messaging, caching, and containerization. However, the answer remains high-level and lacks concrete examples or hands-on project details, which limits the completeness of the response. Important practices such as security implementation, detailed performance optimization techniques, and deployment best practices like CI/CD pipelines are missing. The communication is somewhat repetitive and could be clearer, but the overall technical correctness and relevance are sufficient to consider this a passing but not strong answer.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate shows solid conceptual knowledge of scalable system architecture in Java and Spring Boot and touches on relevant tools like Kafka, Docker, and Redis. However, the response is generalized with limited specific experience or details. More depth and examples are needed to confidently assess practical skills."},{"prompt":"Can you give me an example of a RESTful API or microservice you’ve designed and developed recently, and what challenges you faced?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":6,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Mentions a specific domain (claims processing system) and relevant API functionalities (claim creation, status, tracking, reservation management, payment workflows).","Includes important technical aspects like validation, role-based authentication, exception handling, pagination, and SQL query optimization.","Demonstrates familiarity with some challenges such as authentication and query performance."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-2","weaknesses":["Details on scalability, reliability, or distributed system challenges (e.g., fault tolerance, eventual consistency) are missing.","No mention of frameworks, specific technologies used (like Spring Boot, database type).","Limited discussion on error handling and logging specifics or integration with downstream consumers/microservices."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":7,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate should describe a recent project involving the design and development of a RESTful API or microservice using Java and frameworks like Spring Boot. They should explain the domain context (e.g., claims processing, e-commerce orders), key functionalities exposed by the API (such as CRUD operations, authentication, pagination), and how they ensured scalability and reliability, including database optimizations or caching strategies. Challenges discussed should include handling authentication and authorization (role-based access), managing data validation, optimizing query performance, dealing with distributed system concerns like eventual consistency or fault tolerance, and implementing efficient error handling and logging. The answer should include concrete technical details that show hands-on experience with designing API endpoints, modeling data, and integrating with downstream consumers or other microservices in a distributed architecture.","candidateAnswer":"Yes, so I developed a system. I built REST API for a claims processing system where the adjusters and the downstream systems consumed some kind of claim details. So the APIs were used for claim creation, claim status, tracking, then reservation management, then payment workflows, and I implemented validation, role-based authentication, exception handling. Also, I did pagination and optimized SQL queries for faster retrieval. So that''s how I implemented it.","missing_concepts":["Use of Java frameworks or technologies (e.g., Spring Boot).","Scalability and reliability strategies (caching, database optimizations beyond SQL query tuning).","Handling distributed systems concerns such as eventual consistency or fault tolerance.","More detailed error handling and logging mechanisms.","Integration approaches with downstream consumers or other microservices."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate provided a relevant and technically correct overview of a REST API designed for a claims processing system with key endpoints and functionalities. They mentioned validation, role-based authentication, exception handling, pagination, and query optimization, indicating practical experience. However, the answer lacks depth in describing the frameworks or technologies used, detailed challenges faced, and how they ensured scalability, reliability, or handled distributed system complexities. Including such details would demonstrate stronger understanding and problem-solving skills. Overall, the candidate shows good knowledge but with room for more completeness and technical depth.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate described a REST API for claims processing with key features and some technical details, indicating hands-on experience. However, they fell short of elaborating on frameworks, scalability, distributed system challenges, and deeper problem-solving approaches. The response met the minimum expectations for the role but could be improved with more technical specifics and challenge discussion"},{"prompt":"How did you approach the integration of that API with downstream systems or external services?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":7,"problem_solving":8,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":8},"strengths":["Good understanding of error handling strategies including retries and circuit breakers.","Awareness of loose coupling and improved maintainability by avoiding calling downstream APIs directly from controllers.","Consideration of request/response schema, authentication mechanisms, rate limits, SLA, and timeout handling.","Use of asynchronous messaging (Kafka) for scalability and decoupling.","Implementation of logging and tracing with correlation IDs and tool usage (Splunk)."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-3","weaknesses":["Lacked explicit mention of secure data transmission (e.g., HTTPS).","Did not discuss versioning strategies for backward compatibility.","Less emphasis on RESTful design principles or specific API contract validation.","Some communication was slightly verbose and could be more concise."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":8,"expectedAnswer":"I start by thoroughly understanding the API''s contract and data formats to ensure compatibility with downstream systems. I design the integration using RESTful principles, implementing robust error handling and retries to handle transient failures. For external services, I use standardized authentication mechanisms like OAuth or API keys and ensure secure data transmission via HTTPS. Additionally, I leverage asynchronous messaging or event-driven patterns where applicable to decouple systems and improve scalability. Throughout, I emphasize logging, monitoring, and use versioning strategies to maintain backward compatibility.","candidateAnswer":"So in order to integrate that API with the downstream systems, what we have to think about is we want to make sure that if there is any error or failure, it gets tracked. So we have to take care of that as well. So I made sure that this system makes that design much more flexible in a way that this thing will be taken care of. So first of all, I understood the downstream system that I have in place. Then I also tried to understand the request and response schema that it would generate, then the authentication mechanism. Then I tried to understand the rate limits, SLA and time executions. And at the same time, I also looked at error handling logic, plus what kind of structure I would like in order to generate that. So I avoided calling the downstream APIs directly from the controllers. And this helped me to improve the maintainability and testability. At the same time, also make sure that my code is loosely coupled. And then I used like a web client for interacting with the third-party systems because a web client would make it much more faster and better compared to the REST templates. So that was one of the things that I did. And I added a at some kind of timeout handling. This helped me to slow down the downstream systems because a lot of times the downstream systems get slowed down and can cause some kind of exhaustion at the thread level. So this would make sure that our system works correctly. Also, I added a circuit breaker in my system that helped me prevent any kind of failure situation. That was one of the things that I did. And retry, like logic I implemented for timeout situations. And definitely logging and tracing is a very, very important part. Like we have to add request correlation IDs, then API latency. So all these things were implemented. And for logging and tracing, I used Splunk, which is a very important tool that I have worked on. And then error handling is also very important. So in my code, I did it using try-catch blocks. And instead of using or exposing the raw errors, I did use some global error handling system in my approach. So that was very good. And for heavy loads, if I wanted to use a stream or some kind of asynchronous communication, I made it through Kafka. In Kafka, I made sure that we are making the system much more scalable, loosely coupled, and fault tolerant. So that''s how I implemented the whole system.","missing_concepts":["Secure data transmission (HTTPS).","Versioning strategies for maintaining backward compatibility.","Explicit mention of following RESTful API design principles.","Specific details on how API contracts were validated."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrated a solid and technically correct understanding of API integration with downstream systems, addressing key aspects such as error handling with retries and circuit breakers, using asynchronous messaging for scalability, and implementing logging and tracing. Their approach to loose coupling, timeout management, and use of appropriate HTTP clients was well explained and relevant. However, the answer missed mentioning secure data transmission practices like HTTPS and strategies for API versioning to maintain backward compatibility, which are critical in real-world integration scenarios. The communication was generally clear but could be improved for conciseness. Overall, this is a strong answer showing good problem solving and relevant knowledge.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate showed a good grasp of API integration concepts, emphasizing robust error handling, scalability through asynchronous messaging, and maintainability. Minor gaps in discussing security and versioning reduce completeness but not enough to fail. Recommend advanced Java and microservices fit."},{"prompt":"Can you tell me about your experience working with Neo4j or any graph-based technologies in your projects?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":0,"completeness":0,"problem_solving":0,"communication_clarity":0,"technical_correctness":0},"strengths":[],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-4","weaknesses":["No answer provided","No demonstration of knowledge or experience with Neo4j or graph technologies","Cannot assess technical skills or problem solving without an answer"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":0,"expectedAnswer":"I have hands-on experience designing and implementing graph data models using Neo4j for applications requiring complex relationship queries, such as social networks and recommendation engines. My work involved defining nodes, relationships, and properties aligned with domain concepts, alongside writing efficient Cypher queries for pattern matching and traversals. For example, in a project focused on knowledge graphs, I modeled entities and their semantic relationships to enable advanced querying and inference. I also integrated Neo4j with Java-based microservices using Spring Data Neo4j, ensuring scalable API access. This experience includes performance optimization, understanding graph index usage, and utilizing Neo4j features like transactions and ACID compliance within distributed architectures.","candidateAnswer":"","missing_concepts":["Any mention of graph database concepts such as nodes, relationships, properties, and queries","Graph data modeling experience","Usage of Cypher query language","Integration with Java or microservices","Performance optimization and ACID compliance in Neo4j context"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate did not provide any response to the question about their experience with Neo4j or graph-based technologies. Without an answer, it is impossible to assess their technical correctness, completeness, relevance, clarity, or problem-solving abilities related to graph databases. It is important to provide at least some information or examples to demonstrate relevant experience and understanding in such technical interviews.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate failed to answer the question regarding Neo4j or graph technologies. No assessment possible due to lack of response."}]'), ('cmp6y6wiv001gnx0kx8rfuyrx', 'cmp6xv92v000pnx0k28756ltz', 96, 100, 90, 100, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 13:23:14.6', '2026-05-15 13:23:14.6', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello.","Hello, can you hear me?","Hello?"]', 'heuristic-immediate', null, null, null), ('cmp6zrqjc002fnx0k694yhu45', 'cmp6z6r0f001knx0kbdqeza9g', 99, 100, 100, 94, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 14:07:26.232', '2026-05-15 14:07:26.232', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["então","Sure, you know, let''s talk about some of the projects. So in my current client, you know, that is a Home Depot, I''ve been focusing on the agent data pipelines that handles a lot of operational stuff, you know, without any constant human intervention. The big project is like I''ve been creating the intelligence system that mounts our third-party vendor feeds. Basically, we get like product catalog updates, pricing changes, and inventory levels from hundreds of suppliers. And you know, the quality is everywhere. So I''m going to use like LangChain and LangGraph. It''s kind of to build the agents that can detect any anomalies, figuring out, you know, what''s going on wrong. In some cases, I need to be fixing the issues as well, based on the root, to an example right there. So and also, you know, the other piece is to handle the real-time pipelines in the Apache airflow, because that moves our data mainly into our snowflake warehouse, mainly into operational systems. And we are going to, you know, pushing the aggregate inventory as well into our Salesforce from the sales operational team. So there will be a rise of the stock levels when the teams is going to be working with different, different clients. And you know, this involves the Mulesoft integrations as well with the different clients that involves a lot of, you know, Mulesoft integrations, I would say, to customer.","So, you know, when we talk about this context, mainly let''s talk about the data quality challenges first, because, you know, we are dealing with a lot of vendor feeds and the building intelligence as well into our pipeline. So when I am building these agents, this will detect, you know, the anomalies and fix the issues. So I''ve been, you know, approaching the different things like confident thresholds, because that''s gonna decide what''s get routed, you know, to a human where it''s fixed. So on the real-time pipeline, I''ll be going with the airflow that''s gonna, you know, pushing our Salesforce into the difference framing, and it''s a more kind of a microbatch setup. So, you know, I''m very curious to set up an airflow. That''s kind of batch oriented. So I was wondering, you know, to use if any airflow sensors. That''s gonna trigger, you know, automatically. And I''ll be going with the MuleSoft integrations as well. This kind of, you know, transformation logic mainly built within the MuleSoft itself, or I will be going with the heavy lifting in Python, you know, tons of MuleSoft here."]', 'heuristic-immediate', null, null, null), ('cmp70aeae003knx0kzsr9ardc', 'cmp701p00002lnx0km27w4j2e', 73, 95, 80, 100, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 14:21:56.823', '2026-05-24 10:25:49.815', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Well, in that case, it was a little hard because we have a lot of spillovers, sprint by sprint, that happens until the team gets the maturity to understand and move together into the transformation. But at the beginning, we need to plan a little bit lower than we used to have. And also, we need to escalate a lot of topics with the different layers that we have. But when the team gets familiar, then we start using better estimations and we reduce the spillovers.","In both cases, I have worked with different dashboards with different metrics, but the main metrics are the velocity, predictability, and all the items that are completed versus the planning at the beginning of the sprint.","If we are going, if we can go into more detail, we can, I have created dashboards for tracking specific activities for each developer or tester, and or a user story that we have for every iteration and the status for them."]', 'question-grade-hybrid', 'gpt-4.1-mini', 62, '[{"prompt":"Please introduce yourself and tell me a bit about your current role.","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":7,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Candidate has solid experience with Scrum Master role over 10 years.","Demonstrates understanding of Scrum Master responsibilities within agile frameworks.","Explains dual role as Scrum Master and Project Manager, indicating breadth of experience.","Communication is generally clear and understandable."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-0","weaknesses":["Lacks specific mention of certifications to validate Scrum knowledge.","Does not reference working with Product Owners or stakeholder alignment.","No explicit discussion of Agile tools such as Jira or Azure DevOps.","Explanation of Scrum Master responsibilities is vague and generic.","Does not highlight leadership level experience or cross-functional team management."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"An ideal candidate would succinctly summarize their background emphasizing relevant Scrum Master experience, certifications, and current responsibilities aligned with the Lead Scrum Master role. For example: \\"I am a Certified Scrum Master with over 8 years leading Agile teams in software development environments. Currently, I serve as a Scrum Lead managing multiple engineering teams, facilitating Scrum ceremonies, removing impediments, and driving Agile adoption across the organization. I work closely with Product Owners and stakeholders to align priorities and ensure timely delivery.\\" The answer should also highlight experience supporting cross-functional teams, using Agile tools like Jira or Azure DevOps, and contributing to Agile transformation efforts.","candidateAnswer":"Sure. Okay, so I have experience as a Scrum Master, more or less 10 years of experience, and my career role is a mixed combination between Scrum Master and Project Manager. My responsibilities as a Scrum Master are to help the team in the agile framework, with some scalable activities or actions. And my responsibilities as a Project Manager is to track the project as a high level, ensure that the milestones are achieved, and be sure that we have everything in place for the deliverable that we need to complete.","missing_concepts":["Certified Scrum Master credential or equivalent.","Facilitation of Scrum ceremonies (e.g., sprint planning, retrospectives).","Use of Agile project management tools like Jira or Azure DevOps.","Driving Agile adoption or transformation within an organization.","Interaction with Product Owners and stakeholders to align priorities."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrates a good base level of experience in Scrum Master and Project Manager roles with 10 years tenure, which is positive for a Lead Scrum Master position. However, the answer lacks details that would demonstrate a strong fit for a leadership role in Agile. There is no mention of certifications, specific Scrum practices like ceremonies or impediment removal, or use of Agile tools, which are critical for this role. The answer is relevant and generally clear but remains vague and incomplete in key areas that demonstrate deep understanding and leadership in Agile practices. To improve, the candidate should provide concrete examples of their Agile leadership, mention certifications, elaborate on interactions with Product Owners, and describe how they have driven Agile transformation initiatives.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate shows reasonable experience and role understanding but does not sufficiently detail leadership skills, certifications, or specific Agile practices necessary for a Lead Scrum Master. The answer is relevant and mostly clear but incomplete to justify a pass."},{"prompt":"How many Agile teams have you managed simultaneously?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":4,"problem_solving":5,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Candidate specifies the number of teams managed simultaneously, which directly answers the question.","The response is relevant and understandable."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-1","weaknesses":["Lacks details on how they balanced priorities or facilitated Scrum ceremonies across multiple teams.","Does not demonstrate supporting multiple teams concurrently without compromising quality or delivery.","No mention of tools or methodologies used to manage teams."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate should specify the number of Agile teams they have managed at the same time, typically ranging from two to five or more depending on their experience and the organization''s size. They should also highlight their ability to balance priorities, facilitate Scrum ceremonies, and effectively support multiple teams concurrently without compromising quality or delivery.","candidateAnswer":"In the past I have been working with four different teams simultaneously.","missing_concepts":["Balancing priorities among multiple teams","Facilitation of Scrum ceremonies","Supporting multiple teams concurrently without loss of quality","Use of Agile tools like Jira or Azure DevOps"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate provides a direct answer to the question by stating they have worked with four teams simultaneously, which is within the expected range. However, the answer is very brief and lacks elaboration on key competencies such as managing priorities, facilitating team processes, or ensuring delivery quality across multiple teams. Without demonstrating these aspects, the response does not fully meet the expectations for a lead Scrum Master role. Further details on their approach, tools used, and problem-solving strategies would have improved the evaluation. Overall, the answer is relevant and clear but incomplete in demonstrating necessary skills.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate knows the basic fact of managing multiple teams but does not provide sufficient detail to confirm their capability at a lead level. They fail to elaborate on critical aspects of managing multiple Agile teams effectively, resulting in a borderline fail."},{"prompt":"Can you tell me about an Agile transformation initiative that you led recently?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":6,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":7},"strengths":["Mentions transitioning from waterfall to an Agile framework","References core Scrum ceremonies such as daily standup, planning, retrospective, sprint demo, backlog refinement","Acknowledges addition of longer-term planning similar to PI planning","Shows awareness of scaling Agile practices"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-2","weaknesses":["Answer is somewhat vague and lacks depth","Mentions ''PIJ'' but does not explain what it is","Limited discussion on collaboration with teams and stakeholders","No concrete metrics or outcomes shared","Brief and lacks examples of resolving impediments or promoting continuous improvement"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":7,"expectedAnswer":"An ideal answer would describe leading an Agile transformation involving a shift from waterfall or less mature Agile practices to a scaled Agile framework tailored to the organization''s needs. The candidate should mention specific actions such as introducing or refining Scrum ceremonies (daily stand-ups, sprint planning, retrospectives, backlog grooming), incorporating longer-term planning events like PI planning or similar to align multiple teams, and adapting frameworks like SAFe if applicable. They should highlight working closely with engineering teams and stakeholders to align priorities, resolving impediments, and promoting Agile best practices to drive continuous improvement. Concrete metrics or outcomes, such as improved velocity, delivery predictability, or team engagement, reinforce the answer''s strength.","candidateAnswer":"Yes, well, I was part of one transition between waterfall into the agile framework. In this case, we defined a mixed agile framework, so a scrum with some ceremonies as a scalable framework. So we have the daily standup, planning, retrospective, sprint demo, backlog refinement. Plus we add a planning for the next three months, pretty similar to a PI planning. And also a PIJ.","missing_concepts":["Clear description of organizational impact or benefits from the transformation","Collaboration with engineering teams and stakeholders","Use or adaptation of specific scaled Agile frameworks like SAFe","Details on resolving impediments or driving continuous improvement","Specific metrics like velocity improvement or delivery predictability"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrates a basic understanding of Agile transformation concepts, describing a shift from waterfall to an Agile approach incorporating standard Scrum ceremonies and some scaled elements similar to PI planning. However, the response lacks clarity, depth, and completeness. They mention ''PIJ'' without explanation and do not provide details about collaboration, impediment resolution, or success metrics. The answer is relevant but somewhat vague and does not fully illustrate leadership in Agile transformation or measurable improvements. To pass, candidate should provide more specific examples and outcomes and clarify ambiguous terms.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate shows foundational knowledge of Agile transformations with recognition of Scrum ceremonies and scaled planning. However, the answer lacks sufficient completeness and clarity to confirm a solid grasp of leading such initiatives, missing key points like stakeholder engagement and concrete results. Candidate does not meet the expected standard for a Lead Scrum Master role in this regard."},{"prompt":"How did you handle repeated sprint spillovers or missed commitments during that transformation?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":8,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Acknowledges the challenge of repeated spillovers during early transformation phases","Mentions planning with reduced scope and escalation of issues","Recognizes team maturity as a factor for improved estimations and reduced spillovers"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-3","weaknesses":["Lacks details about root cause analysis or how impediments were identified and removed","No mention of backlog refinement or improving estimation techniques in a structured way","Does not describe collaboration with leadership beyond ''escalate topics''","Limited explanation of continuous improvement practices or adapting sprint scope beyond initial planning"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"To handle repeated sprint spillovers or missed commitments, I first conducted root cause analysis with the team to understand estimation inaccuracies, dependencies, or obstacles causing delays. I implemented stricter backlog refinement and planning sessions to improve estimation and scope clarity. I worked closely with the team to identify and remove impediments early and escalated unresolved risks to leadership promptly. Additionally, I adjusted sprint scope to be more realistic during early transformation phases, fostering continuous improvement in team velocity and predictability over time.","candidateAnswer":"Well, in that case, it was a little hard because we have a lot of spillovers, sprint by sprint, that happens until the team gets the maturity to understand and move together into the transformation. But at the beginning, we need to plan a little bit lower than we used to have. And also, we need to escalate a lot of topics with the different layers that we have. But when the team gets familiar, then we start using better estimations and we reduce the spillovers.","missing_concepts":["Root cause analysis of spillovers","Backlog refinement and planning improvements","Proactive impediment removal process","Structured escalation and risk management","Continuous improvement to team velocity and predictability"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate provides a general overview of handling sprint spillovers by planning for less scope initially and escalating issues through layers of management. However, the answer lacks technical depth and key components like performing root cause analysis, structured backlog refinement, and proactive impediment removal. While the mention of team maturity and better estimations over time is good, it is vague and unsupported by specific actions taken. Overall, the response shows awareness but insufficient detailed understanding or professional explanation of the process expected from a Lead Scrum Master.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate demonstrates a basic awareness of challenges in sprint spillovers during transformation and acknowledges planning scope reduction and escalation practices. However, the answer is vague and omits critical aspects like root cause analysis, backlog refinement, and continuous improvement processes. Their communication is somewhat clear but lacks depth and structured problem-solving insig"},{"prompt":"What Jira or ADO dashboards did you create for leadership tracking?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":7,"completeness":4,"problem_solving":5,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Candidate identified key metrics like velocity and predictability which are relevant to leadership tracking.","Candidate mentioned comparing completed items versus sprint planning, showing some understanding."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-4","weaknesses":["Answer lacks specifics on dashboard types such as burn-down or cumulative flow charts.","No mention of specific Jira or Azure DevOps features or customization details.","Did not mention tracking impediments, risks, or other leadership-focused metrics as per the ideal answer."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"I created customized Jira and Azure DevOps dashboards focused on key leadership metrics such as sprint velocity, sprint predictability, and progress against planned sprint goals. These dashboards included burn-down and burn-up charts, cumulative flow diagrams, and reports on story and defect status to provide real-time visibility into team performance. Additionally, I built dashboards tracking impediments and risks to highlight blockers affecting delivery timelines. The dashboards were tailored to support leadership in monitoring sprint progress, team capacity utilization, and alignment with release objectives, facilitating informed decision-making and proactive risk management.","candidateAnswer":"In both cases, I have worked with different dashboards with different metrics, but the main metrics are the velocity, predictability, and all the items that are completed versus the planning at the beginning of the sprint.","missing_concepts":["Specific Jira or ADO dashboard types (burn-down, burn-up, cumulative flow diagrams).","Tracking impediments and risks in dashboards.","Tailoring dashboards for leadership decision-making and real-time visibility.","Use of reports on story and defect status."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate shows some understanding of key sprint metrics important for leadership dashboards such as velocity and predictability. However, the answer is vague and incomplete, missing important aspects like specific dashboard types used, inclusion of risk and impediment tracking, and customization details in Jira or Azure DevOps. The explanation lacks depth and breadth needed for a Lead Scrum Master role. Improving specificity and completeness in describing these dashboards would strengthen the response.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate demonstrates a basic understanding of key metrics for leadership tracking but does not provide enough detail on the dashboards created or how they support leadership needs. The answer''s lack of specifics and coverage of comprehensive dashboard features leads to an overall fail at this medium difficulty level."},{"prompt":"How did you resolve conflicts between engineering and business teams during these projects?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":6,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":5,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Recognized the importance of setting up communication channels between teams","Mentioned root cause analysis to understand issues","Highlighted alignment between both sides as goal"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-5","weaknesses":["Did not explain specific techniques or Agile ceremonies used to resolve conflicts","Answer was vague and somewhat incomplete, lacking details on negotiation or balancing priorities","Communication was somewhat unclear and could be better structured to convey approach"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"I resolved conflicts between engineering and business teams by establishing clear, open communication channels to understand each side''s priorities and constraints. I facilitated collaborative discussions to align goals, encouraged transparency around technical impediments and business requirements, and helped negotiate compromises that balanced feasibility with business value. Additionally, I used Agile ceremonies such as sprint planning and backlog grooming to regularly sync expectations and adjust priorities, ensuring both teams felt heard and committed to shared objectives.","candidateAnswer":"If we are going, if we can go into more detail, we can, I have created dashboards for tracking specific activities for each developer or tester, and or a user story that we have for every iteration and the status for them. Well, first of all, I need to set up the communication channels with the business and the tech leads or QA leads to discuss the root cause analysis, understand the input from the business, and go into a midpoint where we can be aligned from both parts.","missing_concepts":["Use of Agile ceremonies like sprint planning and backlog grooming for alignment","Facilitation of collaborative discussions to align goals","Encouragement of transparency around technical impediments and business requirements","Negotiation and compromise balancing feasibility with business value"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrated an understanding of the need to establish communication channels and perform root cause analysis to address conflicts, which shows foundational knowledge. However, the answer lacked depth and specificity, particularly regarding how conflicts between engineering and business were actively resolved using Agile practices, facilitation techniques, and negotiation. The response was somewhat vague and poorly structured, making it harder to assess full understanding and problem-solving capability. To improve, the candidate should provide clearer examples of methodologies and ceremonies used to align both teams and mention how compromises were achieved to ensure shared objectives.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate shows basic awareness of conflict resolution between teams by setting communication channels and understanding issues but fails to elaborate on practical Agile methods or conflict management techniques. The answer requires more detail and clearer articulation to meet expectations for a Lead Scrum Master role."},{"prompt":"What’s one impediment you removed that significantly improved delivery speed?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":7,"problem_solving":8,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":8},"strengths":["Identified communication and handoff timing between developers and testers as a significant impediment.","Clearly explained the solution of incremental handoffs to QA, enabling testers to start work earlier.","Demonstrated understanding of how this change impacted work completion within iterations."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-6","weaknesses":["Answer could be more structured and polished in communication.","Missing explicit mention of related Agile or Scrum methodologies or tools (e.g., Jira, Azure DevOps).","Could better highlight the impact on continuous integration, feedback loops, or overall sprint velocity as per ideal answer."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":8,"expectedAnswer":"An effective impediment removal that significantly improved delivery speed is addressing delays caused by sequential handoffs between developers and testers. By facilitating incremental handoffs as developers complete individual tasks, rather than waiting for entire features to be finished, testers gain sufficient time to start validation earlier, reducing bottlenecks. This approach fosters continuous integration and faster feedback loops, leading to smoother sprint execution and higher throughput.","candidateAnswer":"One of them is communication between the developers and the testers. We are not able, the team was not able to complete 80% of the work that was planned at the beginning of the iteration, and that was because all the developers hand over their work when they are completely done. So this is not what we are expecting, because with that, the testers don''t have enough time to complete the work. So what we place is a handover immediately as every developer completes any task, they hand over to QA so they can share or hand over the work to QA in an incremental way, so that can give enough time for the testers to complete the work.","missing_concepts":["Continuous integration and faster feedback loops.","Use of Agile artifacts or tools to support the change.","Broader context of how this impediment fit into sprint execution."],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate addressed a key impediment around delayed handoffs between developers and testers, which aligns well with the ideal answer. The solution of shifting to incremental handoffs is technically correct and relevant, showing good problem-solving skills. However, the answer lacks depth in discussing Agile methodologies or tools used to facilitate this process and does not clearly articulate the impact on feedback cycles or sprint throughput. Communication is understandable but could benefit from clearer structure and more professional phrasing. Overall, the candidate demonstrates solid practical understanding but could improve on completeness and articulation.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate provided a relevant and technically correct example of removing an impediment that improved delivery speed by enabling incremental handoffs between developers and testers. Answers showed good problem-solving and understanding of workflow improvements but lacked mention of methodologies and explicit impact on sprint dynamics. Passed with a good overall score, suitable for a Lead Scrum Mas"},{"prompt":"How have you handled dependencies across multiple Scrum teams?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":7,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":5,"communication_clarity":6,"technical_correctness":6},"strengths":["Recognizes need for communication and escalation between Scrum Masters","Mentions scheduling calls and sharing inputs to manage dependencies","Acknowledges updating relevant stakeholders about impediments"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-7","weaknesses":["Lacks mention of structured coordination methods like Scrum-of-Scrums","No reference to use of Agile tools (Jira, Azure DevOps) for tracking dependencies","Does not detail collaboration with Product Owners or prioritization processes","Answer is somewhat vague and lacks detail on proactive dependency management"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":6,"expectedAnswer":"Handling dependencies across multiple Scrum teams involves proactive coordination and clear communication channels. Typically, this includes regular synchronization meetings such as Scrum-of-Scrums to identify and resolve cross-team dependencies early. Using Agile tools like Jira or Azure DevOps, teams can visualize and track dependencies explicitly via linked tasks or shared backlogs. Collaborating closely with Product Owners and Scrum Masters to prioritize dependent work streams and escalate impediments promptly ensures aligned delivery. Additionally, fostering open communication and providing transparency to all stakeholders help prevent delays arising from inter-team dependencies.","candidateAnswer":"Well, we have different channels of communication, but one of them is that we need to start the escalations, we need to share the inputs with other Scrum masters, we need to schedule the calls, and also we need to keep the RT in touch and updated on every step that we are doing related to the impediments.","missing_concepts":["Scrum-of-Scrums or regular synchronization meetings","Use of Agile tools to visualize and track dependencies","Active prioritization and escalation with Product Owners","Fostering transparency and open communication across teams"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate demonstrates a basic understanding of handling dependencies across Scrum teams by highlighting communication, escalations, and keeping stakeholders updated. However, the answer lacks critical details expected at a Lead Scrum Master level, such as structured synchronization mechanisms (e.g., Scrum-of-Scrums), explicit use of Agile tools to track dependencies, collaboration with Product Owners for prioritization, and fostering transparency. The response is somewhat vague and incomplete, indicating partial understanding but missing key elements necessary for effective cross-team dependency management.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate shows some grasp of dependency management concepts, mainly through communication and escalation channels. Yet, they miss important industry best practices and tools commonly used for coordination among multiple Scrum teams. Overall, the answer is incomplete and does not fully demonstrate the depth of knowledge expected for the role."},{"prompt":"Have you worked as a Lead before? What was the team size?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":5,"problem_solving":6,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":8},"strengths":["Indicates experience as a lead","Specifies team size of 15 members","Mentions team was split into four teams, showing some understanding of team structure"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-8","weaknesses":["Does not describe responsibilities or role specifics as a Lead Scrum Master","Lacks detail about Agile ceremonies or coordination","No mention of tools like Jira or Azure DevOps or methodologies used"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":7,"expectedAnswer":"An ideal candidate has experience serving as a Lead Scrum Master or Scrum Lead, managing teams typically ranging from 5 to 15 members. They should describe the team structure, such as multiple cross-functional teams if applicable, and highlight their responsibilities overseeing Agile ceremonies and team coordination at that scale.","candidateAnswer":"Yes, I have been working as a lead in the past, and my team was of 15 members split in four teams.","missing_concepts":["Responsibilities overseeing Agile ceremonies","Team coordination at scale","Use of Agile/Scrum methodologies","Mention of tools/technology like Jira or Azure DevOps"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate confirms experience working as a lead with a team size that matches the expected range and mentions splitting the team into four teams, which is relevant. However, the answer is quite brief and lacks detail about the candidate''s specific role, responsibilities, and how they managed the teams. There is no mention of Agile ceremonies, Scrum leadership duties, or tools and methodologies which are important for this role. The explanation is clear but minimal, indicating only a partial understanding of the Lead Scrum Master''s responsibilities.","interviewer_summary":"Candidate has relevant lead experience with appropriate team size but provides minimal detail on role responsibilities and Agile practices. Passable answer with room for improvement in completeness and depth."},{"prompt":"How did you handle situations with multiple missed commitments across those teams?","result":"Fail","scores":{"relevance":5,"completeness":3,"problem_solving":3,"communication_clarity":5,"technical_correctness":4},"strengths":["Mentions tracking action items and minutes","Indicates some form of daily follow up"],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-9","weaknesses":["No mention of root cause analysis or collaboration with teams","Does not describe facilitation of retrospectives or discussions","No reference to removing impediments, adjusting sprint scope, or capacity planning","Lacks details about using tools like Jira or Azure DevOps for tracking","No mention of communication with stakeholders or transparency"],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":4,"expectedAnswer":"When multiple teams miss commitments, a strong Scrum Master first conducts a root cause analysis in collaboration with the teams to understand underlying blockers or dependencies causing delays. I facilitate transparent discussions during retrospectives or separate meetings to identify improvements and re-align priorities. I then work cross-functionally to remove impediments, adjust sprint scope if necessary, and reinforce realistic capacity planning. Continuous tracking of these issues through Jira or Azure DevOps helps monitor progress and prevent recurrence. Effective communication with stakeholders ensures expectations are managed and transparency is maintained throughout the resolution process.","candidateAnswer":"Okay, well, I need to track them in a specific way, so I create minutes, I create action items, and I track all of them during my day.","missing_concepts":["Root cause analysis of missed commitments","Facilitation of retrospectives or dedicated meetings","Cross-functional impediment removal","Sprint scope adjustment and realistic capacity planning","Use of Agile tools (Jira, Azure DevOps) for continuous tracking","Effective stakeholder communication and expectation management"],"detailed_feedback":"The candidate''s response is very brief and focuses only on tracking action items and minutes daily. While this shows some level of organization, it fails to address the deeper aspects of handling multiple missed commitments such as performing root cause analysis collaboratively with teams, facilitating transparent discussions, and actively removing impediments. The answer lacks any mention of adjusting sprint scope or capacity planning to prevent recurrence. Also, no specific tools or Agile frameworks are referenced to manage ongoing tracking and communication with stakeholders. Overall, this answer demonstrates only a superficial understanding and does not cover the key responsibilities expected of a lead Scrum Master in this scenario.","interviewer_summary":"The candidate understands the need for tracking tasks but lacks comprehensive Agile and Scrum practices necessary to manage multiple missed commitments effectively. Their approach is basic and does not show strong problem-solving or facilitation skills required at the lead Scrum Master level."}]'), ('cmp71ds1y005anx0kdsm1g65z', 'cmp70s8to004dnx0kr6xvyaz5', 100, 100, 100, 98, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 14:52:34.246', '2026-05-15 14:52:34.246', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello?","So one of the major issues I would say in my recent project, we had recurring failing in high volume nightly ETL jobs caused by script joins and insufficient parsing. I analyzed Spark execution logs, identifying the bottlenecks, and redesigned the transformation logic and optimized joins and tuned the cluster configuration. As a result, the ETL runtime was reduced significantly, and pipeline stability improved and manual operational intervention was minimized.","I worked with Snowflake as a cloud data warehouse, supporting the enterprise analytics and reporting workloads. I worked with including loading curated data sets from the Databricks into Snowflake, writing optimized SQL queries, and creating reporting data sets, and supporting Power BI dashboards for analytics users. I also worked with structured and semi-structured data formats such as JSON and Paracet and supporting data validation and optimization and performance tuning activities."]', 'heuristic-immediate', null, null, null), ('cmp72tce9006fnx0kfm8es4zi', 'cmp72adee005gnx0kncec3bke', 100, 100, 100, 100, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 15:32:40.065', '2026-05-15 15:32:40.065', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Hello?","Hey, how are you?","Yeah, I''m currently working as a, like I said, I''m Srinivas Kalyan Yellanki. I''m currently working as a Senior Software Engineer at Expedia. I work into a team called Incentives and Personalization Platform team, which is like a backend as well as a data platform team. So what we do here in the team is we build personalization signals that can be used within the Expedia, within the teams within the Expedia where they can, you know, you know, like look at the, so basically we provide the users personalized signals which can be used by these teams within the Expedia so that, you know, they can use this information for showing better travel traveler recommendations. So we expose this data from our team through GRPC API as well as the SDK. And the tech stack that we use is Java, Kotlin for development of this GRPC API as well as the SDK. We use Kafka as a streaming platform and the we use Apache Spark for data batch processing. And I think, yeah, pretty much that''s like a tech stack. Cassandra is a data store, by the way. And prior to this, I worked at various other companies, but this is my current work at Expedia."]', 'heuristic-immediate', null, null, null), ('cmp740mru0077nx0k82n189o6', 'cmp73weva006jnx0kwaqagj18', 57, 61, 52, 58, 'Needs more concise and evidence-backed responses. Practice follow-up handling.', '2026-05-15 16:06:19.723', '2026-05-15 16:06:19.723', '["Stayed engaged through interview","Attempted structured responses"]', '["Use concrete examples with outcomes","Answer more directly before elaborating"]', '["Hi, good morning. This is Navya Krishna Bandaru. I am working as a senior data state data engineer with 10 plus years of experience as ETL and, you know, Snowflake. I have used different kinds of cloud platforms like Azure, AWS, GCP, and I do have experience towards Python, SQL, Snowflake as well. And I am working as, currently I''m working as a senior data engineer, and I have worked, experienced towards multiple domains like mortgage, higher education, healthcare, banking, and insurance and finance as well. I work with data quality, maintaining the data quality, data integration, data maintaining the pipeline management, pipeline monitoring, and as well as the data ingestion. And I do have experience towards Snowflake, SQL, Python, and DB2, Oracle, all these databases. And this role suits my profile because I have seen the profile indicates it is related to the Informatica, which I do have experience in the Snowflake and Python as well. So this is what about me.","I was told by the interviewer this is related to the snowflake and this is related to all the data engineer positions having experience towards the snowflake and the cloud platforms."]', 'heuristic-immediate', null, null, null), ('cmp74gwv2007rnx0kmei6mqzt', 'cmp73wydu006unx0kqei6jv8q', 82, 100, 100, 86, 'Strong performance with structured answers and solid topic understanding.', '2026-05-15 16:18:59.294', '2026-05-24 10:19:49.897', '["Clear communication flow","Good depth in responses"]', '["Add sharper quantified examples","Reduce filler and tighten transitions"]', '["Sure, so in my recent project, right, so basically I am a full-stack Java developer, so I have experience in complete software development lifecycle from designing, developing, testing, deploying all the complex applications into the cloud platforms as well. So I worked on backend as well as front-end technologies. So when it comes to the backend part, I worked with Java. I have good experience in building microservice architectures. I have strong knowledge in OOPs concepts, core Java concepts, and also I worked on function interfaces, Lambda expressions, stream APIs, and advanced Java features. And also I have used Spring Boot framework, like I worked with Spring MVC, Spring Data JPA, Spring Cloud, Spring Security, where I have implemented both JDBC and OAuth2 for authentication and authorization purpose. And also I have used Spring AMQP as well while working with RabbitMQ and Kafka. In addition to that, I worked on REST APIs and GraphQLs. So along with that, I have very good experience with reactive programming to perform asynchronous operations using Flux and Mono. And on the front-end part, so I worked with HTML, CSS, JavaScript, TypeScript, and also I worked with UI frameworks like React as well as Angular. So I have leveraged all the complex concepts such as I have worked with services, dependency injections, guards, pipes, and all those things. So and also I am proficient using RxJS operators as well. So basically I have worked with structured databases as well as unstructured databases. Like I have used SQL Server, Oracle, MySQL, and also unstructured databases like MongoDB, RedisDB, and also Cosmos DB. I worked especially on GCP platform where I have utilized like I have used and also I worked on AWS as well, like where I have used CloudRun, App Engine, and also I worked on Firestore and all these things. And also I have worked with Terraform as well. So coming to the CI-CD part, so I have used Dockers and Kubernetes to create and manage containers which are used to run the microservices. And also I have used Jenkins for continuous integration and delivery purpose. And also I have used SonarCube and JUnit for code quality and testing purpose. And I have also optimized SQL queries as well. So coming to my previous project, I recently worked with Truest Bank. So here the implementation partner is Cognizant. So here I have worked on migrating a monolithic application to a microservice-based architecture through focusing on the payment service. So here we handled various types of transactions including real-time ACH payments, credit card payments, debit card transactions, as well as wire transfers, P2P transfers, recurring payments, bill payments, and also the mobile wallet transactions as well. So here my primary responsibility was managing all the real-time ACH transactions, like ensuring the secure and efficient processing. So we have developed specialized microservices to handle different aspects of ACH transactions. Like we mainly worked on ACH transaction service. So this is the core service that processes ACH payments including direct deposits, direct debits, P2P transfers, batch processing, and also the recurring payments. So here I have used Spring Security with OAuth2 and JWT to ensure proper authentication and authorization and also to ensure that only authorized users could access the sensitive information. So for the API traffic management, I have utilized Amazon API Gateway, which helped in securing the endpoints as well as load balancing. So when it comes to this payment service, right, so I have used Spring Batch along with executor service. And also I have used completable features to process batch transactions in parallel and in a thread-safe manner. So for recurring payments, I have utilized a cross-scheduler to run the scheduled tasks at regular intervals of time. And to manage the system''s data, I have used a mix of relational databases as well as NoSQL databases. Like we have used PostgreSQL and Oracle and also used MongoDB and RedisDB. And also I have used Apache Kafka for implementing the event-driven messaging systems. So we have enabled asynchronous and reliable message processing to handle the notification services, transaction logs, and also the real-time updates. And also I have implemented Saga design pattern to ensure the transactions failed, earlier steps could be rolled back with the help of compensating transactions. And to optimize the performance, I have applied the CQRS pattern separating the commands from the queries, so allowing for better handling of high-write and high-read operations. So in addition to that, I used Circuit Breaker pattern with Resilience4J. So this is my overall introduction and also what I have been working in my previous project.","So when it comes to that, right, so yeah, I have built a microservices, so in my recent project, and also we have used this for building a real-time ACH transaction service. So here we have specialized a microservices which can handle like different aspects of ACH transaction. So we have our ACH transaction service. So this is one of the core microservices that I would say because it is used to manage the direct deposits, direct debits, P2P transfers, batch processing, and also the recurring payments. It manages the payment completion and it retries the failed transactions and also it handles if there are any issues as well. So the service also connects with the banks and also the financial institutions to process all the payments. So this microservice, right, so we have used different microservice patterns like we have used a circuit breaker pattern, we have used saga design pattern, and also we have used API gateway design pattern. So these are the major patterns that I have used. So when it comes to the Node.js, right, so I have used Node.js as well. So in this project, so.","So when it comes to the Node.js part, right, so in this microservices, so here, so we have basically responsible for developing some of these microservices using Node.js because this Node.js is very good for handling all the asynchronous and also the event-driven workloads as well. So for example, in our ACH processing service, right, so here many operations are IO heavy, which are like input and output heavy operations are to be made, so such as like calling bank APIs, publishing Kafka messages, reading the transaction status as well as sending notifications and also writing the audit logs. So here Node.js, right, so it basically helped us to handle all these operations efficiently using its non-blocking event loop."]', 'question-grade-hybrid', 'gpt-4.1-mini', 72, '[{"prompt":"Could you give me a brief introduction about your current role and the kinds of projects you’ve been working on recently?","result":"Pass","scores":{"relevance":9,"completeness":9,"problem_solving":8,"communication_clarity":7,"technical_correctness":9},"strengths":["Detailed explanation of technical stack including Java, Spring Boot, microservices, and cloud platforms.","Mentioned domain-relevant technologies like RabbitMQ, Kafka, OAuth2, JWT, and API Gateway.","Included project specifics such as payment service, ACH transactions, and migration from monolith to microservices.","Covered several software development lifecycle stages and CI/CD tools.","Demonstrated understanding of design patterns like Saga, CQRS, and Circuit Breaker."],"difficulty":"medium","questionId":"txq-0","weaknesses":["Answer was somewhat verbose and occasionally redundant, impacting communication clarity.","Limited reference to AI-driven systems or LLMs as cited in the ideal answer.","No direct mention of Neo4j or graph database technologies, although some NoSQL databases were referenced.","Front-end discussion, while thorough, was somewhat tangential given the question’s focus on current role and backend projects."],"isMandatory":true,"overallScore":8,"expectedAnswer":"A strong candidate should outline their current position as a Software Engineer focused on backend development, primarily using Java and/or Node.js. They would mention experience leading or contributing to projects involving the design and implementation of scalable microservices using Spring Boot or Node frameworks. Key projects should include building RESTful APIs within distributed architectures, potentially integrating graph technologies like Neo4j, and applying domain-driven design principles. They might highlight involvement 